[
  {
    "claim": "Exact source and isolation",
    "finding": "The new workflow, wrapper and harmless Python controls are added validation inputs only. Original Rust, p8_scale_matrix.py, p8_runner_capacity.py, old workflows, registry/guard, Cargo, seed, N, phase list, oracle implementation, physical budget and task definitions remain inherited verbatim. The diagnostic is separately named; its rep0 must never be appended to a pre-existing 150-shard/1500-sample cohort."
  },
  {
    "claim": "Original driver command and measurement boundary",
    "finding": "launch invokes the unchanged driver run for scale 100000, shard-index 0, shard-count 30, repetitions 30 and capacity-profile scale_wide_dirty_v1. This retains wide main4096/resume1024, original 5h native deadline, 512MiB native output limit, original +120s driver timeout, and the original 100k skip_fanout behavior. Fanout8/128 remains the registered contract, not a fabricated observed fanout sample. The workflow uses separate protocol/build targets and original capacity preparation/build producer; no claims of new Rust, native or scale execution."
  },
  {
    "claim": "Finite schedule and actual exit",
    "finding": "Twenty periodic captures are anchored to launch monotonic time at 900-second offsets, rather than chaining twenty sleeps after upload duration. A final preservation step allows the original driver's tail within the existing 350-minute job bound; it is not a replacement five-hour measurement timer. supervisor records actual child exit/signal and original native report if present. Native absent is unknown, never zero. verdict checks observer stability, terminal/no wrapper error or timeout, actual driver0, original shard passed0 and native measurement_complete0. Wrapper process0, process liveness, raw existence or a prefix upload cannot imply native completion."
  },
  {
    "claim": "Process ownership",
    "finding": "The wrapper kills only its own unreaped driver child PID on its outer timeout. It does not use broad pkill, infer ownership of other processes or claim to have cleaned the Rust worker's separate process group. The original Rust supervisor owns that worker. RUNNER_TRACKING_ID remains present for normal job cleanup. Early wrapper failure may leave the native cleanup state unknown; the report says so rather than inventing cleanup success."
  },
  {
    "claim": "Original bytes and bounded local copying",
    "finding": "Each stream capture freezes an fstat size, copies only the newly observed byte interval in bounded blocks, hashes those bytes and retains source/start/end. Partial JSON records are valid raw evidence. Frozen chunk files are not modified; pending bundles use hardlinks, avoiding repeated local body copies. Metadata are explicitly point-in-time raw snapshots, not promised complete or atomic JSON. Capture detects inode/device changes or shrink below the prior offset, preserves old chunks and emits a fault. Such a fault prevents a complete-custody inference; no semantic event or EOF is invented."
  },
  {
    "claim": "Transport acknowledgment",
    "finding": "Only an upload step whose actual outcome is success supplies artifact-id to the next checkpoint; the wrapper also requires a positive ID and a 64-character lowercase digest. Missing/failed acknowledgment does not advance custody. The next finite checkpoint includes unacknowledged chunks again. A manifest identifies all captured chunks, the exact included set and the contiguous acknowledged prefix before its own upload. Final/early-terminal uploads can require the external original Actions artifact receipt to establish their custody, since no later checkpoint may acknowledge them. Local availability is not remote durability."
  },
  {
    "claim": "Native outcome and custody are different",
    "finding": "capture_faults do not deliberately change the native verdict. A native-complete run can still have partial or unknown captured custody. A future reader must inspect every manifest/fault/offset/hash and actual upload receipt, and report native outcome separately from transport completeness. Whole-runner loss proves only the intact prefixes in successfully uploaded artifacts; all later bytes and native terminal state may remain unknown. Missing stream files are not proof of zero output. A stream prefix must not be treated as a complete JSONL/event population."
  },
  {
    "claim": "Necessary supervision-error preservation correction",
    "finding": "The initial v2 periodic STREAMS omitted wrapper.stdout and wrapper.stderr even though launch redirects the background supervisor to these files. Receipt/startup failures could leave their only error there. The final v3 adds exactly these two streams with the same raw-byte capture rules and one harmless preservation control. Initial v2 source, actual P and its 14 controls are retained as prior history; they are not relabeled as v3 execution."
  },
  {
    "claim": "Runtime observer source identity",
    "finding": "Before launch and at finalization the three observer files are hashed as actual bytes, recomputed as Git blobs, and checked against HEAD ls-tree path/mode/OID. Full observer before/after equality is required for verdict0. This observer identity is recorded separately from the unchanged original driver's product/build/source identity. Source-after missing/error/mismatch is not silently accepted."
  },
  {
    "claim": "Real controls and limits",
    "finding": "The reviewer reads the author's exact stdout/stderr/exit and before/after candidate hashes, rather than rerunning tests. Fake subprocess controls exercise real nonzero exit, signal, short outer timeout and standalone supervisor; byte tests cover partial line, missing ACK, invalid digest, truncation, no-clobber, clock and observer rejection. These establish their stated host-only scope, not GitHub upload/network behavior, Rust formatting/compilation, product correctness, timing improvement or successful 100k completion."
  },
  {
    "claim": "Resource and action-version boundaries",
    "finding": "The existing actions/upload-artifact@v4 tag is reused and is not misdescribed as a commit-SHA pin. Capturing and uploading can perturb I/O and scheduling, which is why this is an independent diagnostic source/run. Hardlinks reduce duplicate local payload storage; compression/remote transfer costs are not zero, and repeated failed uploads can cause bounded retransmission over the finite schedule. No new performance threshold or general business acceptance gate is added."
  }
]

